  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining User Needs**\n\nOkay, so I\'m focusing on the user request \"install n8n\". My thought process is centered around creating a solid interactive build application. Specifically, the user needs an \"Interactive n8n Installation & Self-Hosting Hub\". This hub has to include comprehensive installation guides, a one-click configuration generator, and a command-line interface. I think this will satisfy the prompt.\n\n\n**Expanding Installation Options**\n\nI\'ve been expanding on the interactive installation concept. Specifically, I\'m focusing on providing multiple execution tools for installing and running n8n. Docker/Docker Compose is getting an interactive builder to toggle configurations and generate commands. The npm Quickstart includes troubleshooting common issues. Cloud 1-Click Deployments are being added. There will also be Tunnel & Webhook configurations and an Instance Connector.\n\n\n**Designing Node Graph**\n\nI\'m now designing an interactive SVG/Canvas node graph. I\'m imagining draggable nodes with zoom/pan, connection wires, and input/output port handles. I\'m focusing on a full library of essential n8n nodes, broken down into Trigger Nodes, Core Nodes, and Output/Action Nodes, each with real-time execution capabilities. The live execution engine will allow testing workflows and individual steps with visual status feedback.\n\n\n**Adding Workflow Functionality**\n\nI\'m integrating n8n workflow compatibility. This means enabling export to n8n JSON format, so users can transfer workflows to their instances. Importing n8n JSON is also a focus, allowing users to visualize and test their existing workflows within the interactive graph. I\'m adding prebuilt templates to get users started, like an AI Webhook Processor and a Scheduled API Health Monitor. I\'m also including a local webhook endpoint tester.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
