    thinking: "**Analyzing the UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the UI, particularly the sidebar\'s behavior in the user\'s screenshot. It seems the issue stems from `Sidebar.tsx`, where the component uses specific CSS classes which causes the sidebar to take up space on mobile and tablet. I am assessing if there is a conflict.\n\n\n**Refining the Sidebar Logic**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the crux of the UI issue within the sidebar\'s CSS. The problem lies with how the sidebar handles visibility across different screen sizes. I\'m focusing on the interaction between `-translate-x-full` and the element\'s inherent width on non-desktop screens. My assessment now centers on correcting how the sidebar is rendered. I believe the fix is to remove it from document flow on small screens.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Header Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the header\'s responsiveness across different screen sizes, which is causing severe crowding on tablets and mobiles. Specifically, the search bar\'s implementation with `md:block` appears to be a major offender, resulting in truncation on mid-sized screens. I need to restrict the search bar\'s expansion to larger screens or make it compact, and revise the layout of `All Brands` and the status indicators to prevent crowding. My primary focus is improving its legibility on smaller screens by adjusting the UI layout elements.\n\n\n**Evaluating Text Overflows**\n\nI\'m now assessing how text overflows in the header component, specifically the brand name and creator details. On smaller screens, the brand name wraps, causing visual clutter. I\'m considering limiting the brand name\'s length, adjusting the font size, or altering the layout to prevent wrapping and improve readability. The key is to keep all text on a single line.\n\n\n"
