    thinking: "**Considering Mobile Usability**\n\nI\'ve been examining `ContentTable.tsx` and realized that even with `overflow-x-auto`, long tables are tricky on phones. The nine-column table example highlights the problem. The current plan is to add a mobile card view, only showing it on small screens (using `md:hidden` as a starting point).\n\n\n**Analyzing Sidebar Design**\n\nI\'ve been reviewing the sidebar implementation in `Sidebar.tsx`, specifically lines 101-108. The mobile behavior is now crystal clear and efficient: the sidebar hides completely on small screens, using `hidden` and `fixed` positioning, and correctly overlays the screen when opened. This fixes the previous issue of layout-pushing white space.\n\n\n**Evaluating Header Component**\n\nI\'m now diving into `Header.tsx`, specifically the mobile drawer trigger and brand switcher. I\'m focusing on lines like the ones involving the `<button>` for the mobile menu toggle. It\'s looking good, using `lg:hidden` to hide the hamburger icon on larger screens, as expected. I\'m also looking into how the `select` element is being handled for brand selection on mobile, wanting to make sure it\'s intuitive.\n\n\n**Refining Mobile Header**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the brand selection in `Header.tsx`, particularly how the `<select>` is handled for smaller screens. I am making sure it\'s intuitive. I\'m also now reviewing the global search element. I\'m focusing on the `lg:hidden` logic and ensuring the input field\'s appearance and functionality remain optimal for mobile.\n\n\n**Analyzing Header Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the right side of the `Header.tsx` component, specifically the n8n status pill and date range selector. I\'m taking a close look at how these elements are handled across different screen sizes, with an emphasis on mobile. I\'m assessing the effectiveness of the hidden date range selector using `xl:flex`. I\'m also confirming the status pill\'s visual and interactive behavior is appropriate on small screens, particularly the interaction logic.\n\n\n**Reviewing Header Interactions**\n\nI\'m now specifically assessing the interactions within the header, focusing on mobile responsiveness. The date range selector is working well; its behavior makes sense across screen sizes. I am now evaluating the notification bell dropdown and user profile dropdown. My concern is making sure they are intuitive and easy to use on smaller screens, using the correct ref hooks.\n\n\n**Optimizing User Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user display in the header, specifically how it adapts to different screen sizes. I\'ve ensured on smaller screens (less than `md`), the user name doesn\'t consume an excessive 100px of width. On even smaller screens (less than `sm`), the chevron is correctly hidden, prioritizing the avatar to save horizontal space and eliminate wrapping or clipping.\n\n\n**Implementing Mobile Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focused on mobile interaction for post details. The current plan involves implementing a smooth mobile detail view toggle or smooth scrolling. In the post detail card, the goal is to make it intuitive and easy to use on smaller screens. I am starting by ensuring there is good handling of `selectedPost.brandName` and the overall layout.\n\n\n**Refining Detail Actions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the action buttons within the post details, specifically for mobile. I\'m taking a close look at the \"Request Revision\" and \"Reject\" buttons. I\'m focusing on ensuring they are easily tappable and don\'t feel cramped on a small screen. I\'m thinking about the visual hierarchy and ensuring the labels are legible and the actions are clearly separated.\n\n\n\n"
