    thinking: "**Analyzing the User\'s Request**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s explicit request for \"n8n with node x,\" aiming for comprehensive support. My focus is on the nuances of this integration, specifically considering how to build this. I am trying to determine the full scope of what the user is asking, with the goal of providing a robust solution.\n\n\n**Defining the Node Structure**\n\nI\'m now concretizing the structure for \"Node X\" within n8n. I\'m focusing on defining the node with its operations and associated credentials, including OAuth 2.0 and API Key/Secret options. I\'m carefully considering the integration points within the `n8n-nodes-base` structure, specifically looking at `src/types/workflow.ts` and `src/data/nodeDefinitions.ts` files to ensure seamless integration.\n\n\n**Designing the X Node**\n\nI\'m now creating the detailed structure for the X node. I\'m prioritizing testing payloads and default outputs formatted according to the Twitter API v2 JSON responses, to ensure proper data handling. I\'m also planning the necessary icons and styling, including incorporating the minimalist X logo, across the n8n interface for a cohesive user experience. Finally, I\'m designing the NodeInspector panel, defining operation selections and dynamic controls for various operations such as Tweet creation, replies, and searches.\n\n\n**Implementing API Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the real-time node execution. I have created a simulation of Node X execution, using JSON payloads that replicate Twitter API v2 responses, to validate data handling. I\'m also working on a direct credential tester with instant validation and a live status badge within the credentials configuration tab. Finally, I\'ve started a new workflow template named \"X (Twitter) v2 Automated Omnichannel Dispatcher & Thread Orchestrator.\"\n\n\n**Visualizing the Workflow Builder**\n\nI\'m now integrating the visual workflow builder into the UI. I\'m focusing on user access and design. I\'m investigating how to allow users to switch between the orchestration cards and the interactive canvas. I\'m considering using tabs or a toggle for \"Active n8n Pipelines\" vs \"Visual Workflow Canvas (with Node X).\" I\'m also considering an \"Open in Visual Canvas\" button to give users access to a visual interface with `Node X` integration.\n\n\n**Adding Backend Support**\n\nI\'m now integrating backend support for the X node. I\'m adding an n8n workflow mapping to the database and supporting specialized simulation results within `N8nService`. I\'ve started by adding `\'xNode\'` (and aliases) to the `NodeType` within `src/types/workflow.ts` to facilitate this integration. The new structure will facilitate data simulation and workflow integration. My goal is to support testing and workflow execution.\n\n\n**Populating Node Parameters**\n\nI\'m now populating the node definitions with default parameters in `src/data/nodeDefinitions.ts`. I\'ve started by adding an `xNode` entry with an example tweet and associated parameters to make it easy to get started for new users. This includes several parameters, such as the `operation`, `text`, and `mediaUrls`. I\'ve also added examples for Twitter-specific parameters like `searchQuery` and `inReplyToTweetId`. This will give users a head-start with the basic API operations.\n\n\n"
