    thinking: "**Examining Node Execution**\n\nI\'ve got the `NodeInspector` ready for the Workflow Builder! Now I\'m focusing on node execution logic, specifically with `onRunSingleNode` for testing. I\'m looking at `xNode` and its operation `createTweet`. My input parameters include `text`, `mediaUrls`, and `reply`. I\'m starting to visualize how these flow through the system.\n\n\n**Analyzing Output Generation**\n\nI\'m now examining the outputs from the Twitter nodes. I can see the data structures being returned, including the nested data, includes, and rate limits of a successful tweet. The `replyTweet` node\'s structure is also clear, showing how it references the original tweet. I\'m taking note of the different JSON schemas. I can already see the usefulness of these outputs, and am considering next steps for the Workflow Builder.\n\n\n**Developing UI Components**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining the UI for the `xNode` inspector within `NodeInspector.tsx`. I\'m designing a node header with an X logo, a version tag, and an operations selector for actions like `createTweet` or `getUser`. I also plan an authentication section for OAuth types. My goal is to create a more functional and informative UI for users.\n\n\n**Designing Credential Input**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user input fields for API credentials, specifically for Twitter\'s API. I\'m building form fields for API Key, Secret, Access Token, and Bearer Token. I\'m also adding a \"Test Credentials\" button that provides instant validation. I\'m visualizing the green success message with the app ID and rate limits. The next step is operation-specific form fields.\n\n\n\n"
