  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Automations Page**\n\nI\'ve been examining `AutomationsPage.tsx`, specifically focusing on the main visual canvas box. I\'m taking note of the relative positioning, rounded corners, border, shadow, background color, and minimum height definitions. I want to understand how these elements work together to create the desired visual layout and user experience. I\'m trying to see if I can optimize it.\n\n\n**Examining `NodeInspector`**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `NodeInspector` component, specifically focusing on its positioning and how it\'s integrated within the larger Automations Page. The `fixed inset-y-0 right-0 z-40 w-full` class names immediately caught my eye. I\'m assessing how these styles ensure it\'s pinned to the right side and overlays other content, considering z-index and width to ensure proper display. I\'m trying to see if I can optimize this as well.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Visual Impact**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the initial user experience when loading the Automations page. The `NodeInspector` component is immediately visible due to its `fixed inset-y-0 right-0` positioning, taking up the entire vertical space on the right, and covering a significant portion of the page. This is because `selectedNodeId` is pre-set to a value and this triggers the component to appear right away. The main canvas, with its relative positioning, rounded corners, and shadow, is now under review in light of this initial visual impact.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Layout Issue**\n\nI\'ve just uncovered a critical issue in how the `WorkflowCanvas` component calculates its height. When the parent `div` sets only `min-h-[580px]` without an explicit `height` or `flex-1`, the `h-full` style applied to `WorkflowCanvas` resolves to zero or content height, which is also zero because all its children use absolute positioning. Because all of the children of `WorkflowCanvas` are `absolute` positioned, it has no actual internal height! The use of `overflow-hidden` on the parent, or the zero height, causes the empty, black canvas.\n\n\n"
