  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Grid Pattern**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a missing piece: the `canvas-dot-grid` class wasn\'t defined in the CSS. So, I added the dot grid pattern in `src/index.css`. Now, I\'ve got a radial gradient background with specified size to create the desired effect.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Display Issue**\n\nNow, I\'m diving deeper into why the canvas was initially appearing black. It seems the parent `div` in `AutomationsPage.tsx` lacked a specific height declaration, causing the `WorkflowCanvas`\'s `h-full` to resolve to zero height. I\'ve updated the code to explicitly set the height, ensuring the canvas renders correctly. I\'m also examining the `NodeInspector.tsx` to understand why it\'s positioned as `fixed`, which appears to be covering the display.\n\n\n**Refining the Node Inspector**\n\nI\'ve been wrestling with the `NodeInspector`\'s positioning, and the problems it presented. Changing the positioning to `absolute` within the canvas container has dramatically improved the display! Now it stays within the canvas, without covering the header or overlapping other elements. Nullifying `selectedNodeId` on load provides a clear initial view of the canvas. The smooth closing on click of the inspector is a very welcome feature.\n\n\n**Examining Node Positioning**\n\nI\'m now examining how the nodes are positioned within the canvas, specifically looking at the `tpl-x-social-orchestrator` template. I\'ve located the node definitions in `src/data/templates.ts`. The x-coordinates span a wide range, which is critical for defining the layout. I\'ll need to figure out how to handle the positioning of the \"X\" button on the `Node` card.\n\n\n"
